WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.550
There's so many different kinds of life
on earth, but all living things are

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.510
related to each other. Scientists
divide all living things into one of

3
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.600
two groups: eukaryotes and prokaryotes.

4
00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:16.079
At a very basic level, eukaryotes have
cells with many specialized

5
00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.810
compartments inside of them that we
call organelles. Organelles give them

6
00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.390
capabilities that prokaryotes do not
have.

7
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.840
Imagine every species on earth as the
tip of a huge tree's branching roots.

8
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.850
People and dogs are very different, but
if we follow the roots back into the

9
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:38.550
past, we find a common starting point,
or a common ancestor, that wasn't a dog

10
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.200
or a human, but it was still a mammal.
In the same way, mammals and insects

11
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.350
are very different, but they are joined
together as part of the animal kingdom.

12
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.250
If we go further back in time, and I
mean way, way back, back before our

13
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:56.280
ancestors split from the ancestors of
mushrooms or trees, back when the world

14
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.550
consisted of only single celled
organisms. About two or three billion

15
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.860
years ago, a new form of life emerged
that paved the way for all forms of

16
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.410
life that you can see around you, and
many forms of life that you can't see.

17
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.600
This was the birth of eukaryotes.

18
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.570
Scientists think that one day a small
cell that was good at making energy was

19
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.460
devoured by a larger cell. Instead of
destroying the little energy maker, by

20
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:25.750
some stroke of luck, both cells started
working together. The combination of

21
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.940
the small cell's increased energy
production and the larger cell's

22
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.000
protective outer layer allowed both
cells to flourish, and overtime their

23
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.050
descendants evolved organelles with
very specialized functions that almost

24
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.660
all eukaryotes share today. Mushroom,
human and Redwood cells all share a

25
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.080
nucleus that stores their DNA,
lysosomes that break down waste, and

26
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.770
mitochondria which produce energy.
Prokaryotes, like E. coli, have none of

27
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.660
these specialized compartments. The
development of organelles allowed cells

28
00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:59.170
to accomplish newer and different
challenges, like working together to

29
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:04.090
build complex, multicellular organisms,
such as animals, plants and fungi. If

30
00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.530
it hadn't been for that first
partnership between two tiny single

31
00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.750
celled organisms, we certainly wouldn't
be here, and life today would be much

32
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.980
more microscopic than it is.

